{-
   `[T *n]` - Implementation-Containers.md §6.

   Exactly `n` elements, no count stored anywhere, on any target. It is a *type* and not a Repr
   refinement of `[T]`: the two differ in capability rather than in layout, because a fixed array
   cannot grow, and a Repr variant may never change what a type can do (§9).

   The size assertion is the fixture the build order asks for. Everything else here is about the one
   thing that makes the type worth having - it borrows as the same slice a growable array does, so
   every function already written against `[T]` accepts one with no coercion and no specialization.
-}

import Native

-- The whole claim, as a number. A fixed array is its elements and nothing else, so four `Int`s cost
-- exactly four `Int`s: no count, no capacity, no run pointer, no padding at either end.
fn fixedSize() -> I64:
    let xs = [1, 2, 3, 4] :: [Int *4]
    return sizeOf(xs)

-- And it flattens into a record at zero overhead, which is the reason to want it: a record holding
-- one is the array's bytes plus whatever else it holds, with no per-element metadata between them.
data Quad {values: [Int *4], tag: Int}

fn nestedSize() -> I64:
    let q = Quad {values: [1, 2, 3, 4], tag: 0}
    return sizeOf(q) - sizeOf(q.tag)

{-
   It reaches every `[T]` function - §6's "as an immutable argument it produces a slice; as a
   mutable-element argument a mutable slice. Both free, no coercion, no specialization."

   These two are declared over `[T]` and know nothing about fixed arrays. The conversion at the call
   is one address computation and one constant, where a growable array's is two loads: the base is
   the array's own storage and the length is in the type.
-}
fn total(xs: [Int]) -> Int:
    let &sum = 0 :: Int
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < length(xs):
        sum = sum + xs[i]
        i = i + 1

    return sum

fn double(&xs: [Int]) -> {}:
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < length(xs):
        let value = xs[i]
        xs[i] = value + value
        i = i + 1

-- A slice of a fixed array is an ordinary slice, so a subslice of one costs nothing either.
fn middle(xs: [Int]) -> Int = total(xs[1..3])

{-
   The count survives into the parameter, which is what a fixed array is worth having for.

   `p` is a `[Int *2]` and not a slice - `bindingType` in resolve/type_ast.cpp - so this takes one
   pointer where a `[Int]` parameter takes a base and a length, and the two words a caller would
   have assembled to say "two" are not assembled. The conversion to a slice happens where `total` is
   *called* instead, which is where it is actually wanted.

   Three consequences, and the goldens beside this file hold all three: the caller passes the array's
   own storage with no descriptor built, `length(p)` inside `total` is a `countof 2` rather than a
   field read, and `pairSum(quad)` where `quad` is a `[Int *4]` does not compile - two slices are one
   type and that mistake is a read past the end.

   Nothing here needs const generics: `n` is a literal in every position that mentions it.
-}
fn pairSum(p: [Int *2]) -> Int = total(p)

-- The same parameter written mutably, which is the other half of §6 and goes the same way: the
-- element write is through the array's own storage and there is still no descriptor.
fn bump(&p: [Int *2]) -> {}:
    p[0] = p[0] + 1
    p[1] = p[1] + 1

{-
   A fixed array of records, to pin the stride rather than the size.

   `Pad` is nine bytes and sixteen apart, because a record's trailing padding belongs to whoever
   contains it (§10.3) - and two *elements* of an array are exactly the case where nobody may land in
   it. So `[Pad *3]` is forty-eight and not forty-one, and reading element 2 back is what would fail
   if the elements overlapped.
-}
data Pad {wide: I64, narrow: U8}

fn padStride() -> I64:
    let ps = [Pad {wide: 1, narrow: 2}, Pad {wide: 3, narrow: 4}, Pad {wide: 5, narrow: 6}] :: [Pad *3]
    return sizeOf(ps)

fn padRead() -> I64:
    let &ps = [Pad {wide: 10, narrow: 1}, Pad {wide: 20, narrow: 2}, Pad {wide: 30, narrow: 3}] :: [Pad *3]
    return ps[0].wide + ps[1].wide + ps[2].wide

-- Zero elements is a value occupying nothing rather than a case. It has no element to release and
-- no base address to compute, which is what keeps the derived teardown from walking off the end.
fn emptySize() -> I64:
    let none = [] :: [Int *0]
    return sizeOf(none)

fn main() -> Int:
    -- Four `Int`s and nothing else. `Int` is four bytes, so sixteen.
    if fixedSize() != 16 then return -1

    -- The same sixteen inside a record, which is what "flattens at zero overhead" means.
    if nestedSize() != 16 then return -2

    let &xs = [1, 2, 3, 4] :: [Int *4]

    -- Passed where `[Int]` was written, with no conversion the program can see.
    if total(xs) != 10 then return -3
    if middle(xs) != 5 then return -4

    -- Written through a mutable slice, which reaches the array because the descriptor holds its
    -- address and the array's storage *is* its elements.
    double(xs)
    if total(xs) != 20 then return -5

    -- A different length is a different type. The argument keeps that type all the way into the
    -- callee, which is where it becomes a slice.
    if pairSum([7, 8]) != 15 then return -6

    -- And the same through a mutable parameter, written to in place.
    let &pair = [7, 8] :: [Int *2]
    bump(pair)
    if pairSum(pair) != 17 then return -10

    if padStride() != 48 then return -7
    if padRead() != 60 then return -8
    if emptySize() != 0 then return -9

    return total(xs)
